.TH LSDISPLAY 1 "2026-06-04" "lsdisplay 0.2.3" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
lsdisplay \- list connected displays with details and layout diagram
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lsdisplay
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lsdisplay
lists all connected displays/monitors with manufacturer, model, serial number,
resolution, position, rotation, and connector type. It also renders an ASCII art
diagram showing the physical layout of the displays.
.PP
Display identification is performed by parsing EDID data from
.IR /sys/class/drm/*/edid .
Resolution and positioning information is obtained from
.BR xrandr (1),
with fallback to
.BR kscreen-doctor (1)
or
.BR wlr-randr (1)
on Wayland sessions.
.PP
.B lsdisplay
is similar in spirit to
.BR lscpu (1),
.BR lsusb (8),
and
.BR lspci (8),
but for display devices.
.PP
When EDID data is wrong or missing, persistent \fIoverrides\fR can be defined
(per\-display: model, diagonal size, note) with the \fB\-\-override\-*\fR options.
They are stored in a JSON config file and re\-applied automatically on every run.
.PP
.B lsdisplay
can also scan the local network for Smart TVs with
.BR \-\-scan ,
identifying sets such as Samsung TV Plus and recording them as overrides so they
appear with their real model names on subsequent runs.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Display listing
.TP
.B \-\-json
Output all display information as JSON (for scripting).
.TP
.B \-\-short\fR, \fB\-s
Compact output with one line per display.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-layout
Skip the ASCII art layout diagram.
.TP
.B \-\-connected\-only
Only show connected displays (hide disconnected outputs).
.TP
.B \-\-list\-priority
Show the display priority order with GPU mapping.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Disable coloured output.
.SS Smart TV network scan
.TP
.B \-\-scan\fR [\fISUBNET\fR]
Scan the local network for Smart TVs (e.g. Samsung) and auto\-configure them as
displays. Without \fISUBNET\fR the local subnet is auto\-detected; otherwise pass a
CIDR such as \fI192.168.1.0/24\fR. Detected sets are saved to the overrides file so
later runs show their model names.
.SS Display overrides
Override wrong or missing EDID data. Overrides are keyed by a short display id
(e.g.\& \fBSAM7513\fR) and persisted to a JSON config file.
.TP
.B \-\-override\-list
List the currently configured overrides.
.TP
.B \-\-override\-add
Interactively add an override for a detected display (wizard).
.TP
.B \-\-override\-set \fIKEY\fR
Programmatically set an override for display \fIKEY\fR; combine with
.BR \-\-override\-model ,
.BR \-\-override\-diagonal ,
and
.BR \-\-override\-note .
.TP
.B \-\-override\-remove \fIKEY\fR
Remove the override identified by \fIKEY\fR (e.g.\& \fBSAM7513\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-override\-model \fIMODEL\fR
Model name to record (used with \fB\-\-override\-set\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-override\-diagonal \fIINCHES\fR
Diagonal screen size in inches (used with \fB\-\-override\-set\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-override\-note \fINOTE\fR
Free\-form note or description, e.g.\& the room (used with \fB\-\-override\-set\fR).
.SS General
.TP
.B \-\-version\fR, \fB\-V
Show version number and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help\fR, \fB\-h
Show help message and exit.
.SH OUTPUT
The default output consists of two sections:
.TP
.B CONNECTED DISPLAYS
A table listing each display with output name, resolution, position, diagonal size, manufacturer, model, connector type, serial number, rotation, and primary flag.
.TP
.B LAYOUT
An ASCII art diagram showing the relative positions and sizes of all displays with correct proportions.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
.B lsdisplay
.B lsdisplay \-\-short
.B lsdisplay \-\-json
.B lsdisplay \-\-connected\-only
.B lsdisplay \-\-list\-priority
.B lsdisplay \-\-scan
.B lsdisplay \-\-scan 192.168.1.0/24
.B lsdisplay \-\-override\-list
.B lsdisplay \-\-override\-add
.B lsdisplay \-\-override\-set SAM7513 \-\-override\-model QN65QN900B \-\-override\-diagonal 65 \-\-override\-note Salon
.B lsdisplay \-\-override\-remove SAM7513
.B lsdisplay \-\-json | jq '.[].manufacturer'
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /sys/class/drm/card*/edid
EDID binary data for each display output.
.TP
.I /sys/class/drm/card*\-*/status
Connection status for each output port.
.TP
.I ~/.config/lsdisplay/overrides.json
Per\-user overrides and saved Smart TV scan results. Written by the
\fB\-\-override\-*\fR and \fB\-\-scan\fR commands when run as a normal user;
applies to that user only.
.TP
.I /etc/lsdisplay/overrides.json
Machine\-wide overrides, applied to every user. Written when
\fB\-\-override\-set\fR or \fB\-\-scan\fR is run with
.BR sudo
(the per\-user copy is created as well).
.PP
Override keys are the display manufacturer id plus product code (e.g.\&
.BR SAM7513 ).
.B Precedence:
the per\-user file is read first and, if it exists, wins outright \(em the
.I /etc
file is then ignored entirely (the two are
.I not
merged). An override only replaces the model, diagonal and note shown by
.B lsdisplay
for the matching display; it does
.I not
modify
.BR xrandr (1)
or any X11 configuration (there is no
.I /etc/xrandr
involved).
.PP
.B Note (upcoming change):
a future release will additionally support the XDG base directories
(\fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME\fR, \fB$XDG_CONFIG_DIRS\fR and \fI/etc/xdg/lsdisplay\fR),
merge values across config files in XDG order, and demote
.I /etc/lsdisplay/overrides.json
to a deprecated read\-only fallback \(em so the precedence described above will
then change.
.PP
This XDG support is
.B optional
by design: it is enabled only when the optional third\-party module
\fBpyxdg\fR (Debian package \fBpython3\-xdg\fR) is installed, and
.B lsdisplay
falls back to the hardcoded paths above otherwise. The rationale: lsdisplay must
run on any Linux box with nothing but \fBpython3\fR \(em locked\-down production
servers (no PyPI allowed by policy), minimal containers, rescue and live systems,
air\-gapped hosts \(em where a mandatory third\-party dependency would be a
non\-starter. XDG correctness is therefore an enhancement, never a precondition
for simply listing the displays that are plugged in.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xrandr (1),
.BR kscreen-doctor (1),
.BR lsgpu (1),
.BR lspci (8),
.BR lsusb (8),
.BR lscpu (1),
.BR edid-decode (1)
